A view reading a call that writes state renders the call's value.

`deeper(0)` reads nothing, so it has no dependency for an updater to follow,
and it writes state, so the build cannot evaluate it either. html's static
render wrote nothing for it, and its updater was pruned as one with no
dependencies -- which is safe only where the static render already wrote the
value. The span came out empty on every page, with no diagnostic. It is now
written once at startup, like an i18n string, which is the other case of a
value that depends on nothing and still has to be computed where the page
runs.

deny out/none/html/index.html `<span>\s*</span>` -- the words are written, at startup if not at build time

-- main.sngl --
import ui "sngl:ui"

output {
    go {
        bubbletea
        fyne
        gtk4
    }
    kotlin {
        android
    }
    none {
        html
    }
}

var calls = 0

func deeper(d int) int {
    calls += 1
    return d + 1
}

ui.window #home(title="t") {
    ui.text(value="depth {deeper(0)}")
}
